Exactly. Sports is just the cleanest example because the rules and state transitions are obvious. The same architecture generalizes to movies, games, training, education, history, fantasy, horror, romance, sci-fi, therapy-like guided experiences, museums, travel, and entirely new genres.

The generalized stack is:

\text{persistent world}
\rightarrow
\text{agents + rules + memory}
\rightarrow
\text{canonical event stream}
\rightarrow
\text{observer-specific interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{rendered experience}

For movies, that means the “film” no longer has to be a fixed sequence of frames. You could build the world behind the movie first: characters with goals and memories, locations, causal history, relationships, secrets, physics, tone, narrative pressures. Then the movie becomes one chosen traversal through that world.

So instead of:

\text{script} \rightarrow \text{shots} \rightarrow \text{movie}

you get:

\text{world}
\rightarrow
\text{events}
\rightarrow
\text{director policy}
\rightarrow
\text{edit}
\rightarrow
\text{viewer experience}

Then the same underlying story-world can produce radically different experiences: one viewer gets the detective perspective, another the villain, another a child-safe cut, another a horror cut, another a documentary reconstruction, another an emotional-character study, another a three-hour prestige version, another a 20-minute compressed version.

Genres become partly rendering and interpretation policies.

The same canonical event could be rendered as horror, comedy, tragedy, romance, mystery, or documentary depending on camera, pacing, music, information withholding, perspective, editing, and narrative emphasis.

That gives us another strong primitive:

\boxed{\text{World} \neq \text{Story} \neq \text{Experience}}

The world generates possibilities.
The story selects causal paths.
The experience determines how those paths are perceived.

And then you can let the viewer interact without turning it into a conventional game. Maybe they only choose perspective. Maybe they can ask questions. Maybe they can follow side characters. Maybe they can say, “Show me what happened here ten years earlier,” and the system reconstructs that thread from the world state.

So yes—Rendered Window should probably be promoted from a sports architecture into a more general Pantheon primitive:

Build the latent world deeply enough that many valid experiences can be projected from it.

Sports becomes one instantiation. Movies another. Games another. Education another. History another. Entire genres can become viewing operators over persistent worlds rather than fixed media objects.